{number: 126}
{title: O Scared Head, Now Wounded}
{key: C}

{start_of_verse: 1}
O [C] sacred Head, now [F] wounded,
With [C] grief and [G] shame weighed [C] down,
Now scornfully sur-[F] rounded
With [C] thorns, Thine [G] only [C] crown:
O [C] sacred Head, what [F] glory,
What [C] bliss till [G] now was [C] Thine!
Yet, though despised and [F] gory,
I [C] joy to [G] call Thee [C] mine.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: 2}
What [C] Thou, my Lord, hast [F] suffered
Was [C] all for [G] sinners’ [C] gain;
Mine, mine was the trans-[F] gression,
But [C] Thine the [G] deadly [C] pain.
Lo, [C] here I fall, my [F] Savior!
’Tis [C] I de-[G] serve Thy [C] place;
Look on me with Thy [F] favor,
Vouch-[C] safe to [G] me Thy [C] grace.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: 3}
What [C] language shall I [F] borrow
To [C] thank Thee, [G] dearest [C] friend,
For this Thy dying [F] sorrow,
Thy [C] pity [G] without [C] end?
O [C] make me Thine for-[F] ever;
And [C] should I [G] fainting [C] be,
Lord, let me never, [F] never
Out-[C] live my [G] love to [C] Thee.
{end_of_verse}
